{
  "id": "bertotti_robinson",
  "name": "Bertotti-Robinson Electrovacuum",
  "short_name": "Bertotti-Robinson",
  "description": "A conformally flat electrovacuum solution with uniform electromagnetic field, geometrically the product of two spaces of two dimensions each (AdS₂ × S²). It appears as the limit near the horizon of an extremal Reissner-Nordström black hole.",
  "tags": [
    "electrovacuum",
    "electromagnetic field",
    "charged",
    "conformally flat",
    "static",
    "stationary",
    "exact solution",
    "near-horizon limit",
    "extremal",
    "spherically symmetric",
    "Lorentzian",
    "Einstein equations",
    "four-dimensional",
    "idealized model"
  ],
  "signature": "(-,+,+,+)",
  "convention": "Coordinates are $(t, r, \\theta, \\phi)$ in the static chart and $(t, x, \\theta, \\phi)$ in the Poincaré chart, with $t$ a time and $r$ and $x$ lengths, and factors of $c$ and $G$ are kept explicit. Neither $r$ nor $x$ is an areal radius: every sphere of the spacetime has the same areal radius $b$, and $r$ and $x$ run along the AdS$_2$ factor, related by $rx = b^2$. Neither set of coordinates is global. Under $x = b^2/r$ the static coordinates become the Poincaré coordinates, so the two cover one and the same Poincaré patch of AdS$_2$, and the static $r = 0$, which is the Poincaré $x \\to \\infty$, is a degenerate Killing horizon at the edge of that patch rather than the end of the spacetime, which continues through it into the rest of global AdS$_2$. The single parameter $b$ is the common radius of the two factors and is fixed by the uniform electromagnetic field. Components are printed in the chart $x^0 = ct$, so the metric components carry no dimensions, and the dots in the geodesic equations are derivatives of that same chart with respect to an affine parameter, so $\\dot{t}$ means $d(ct)/d\\lambda$. Nothing in the entry depends on $t$, so no published component carries a factor of $c$ at all. The Ricci tensor is the standard contraction $R_{\\mu\\nu} = R^\\alpha{}_{\\mu\\alpha\\nu}$. The Ricci scalar vanishes and the Weyl tensor vanishes, the first because the two factors have equal and opposite curvature and the second because the spacetime is conformally flat, but the Ricci tensor does not: this is an electrovacuum, and the Einstein tensor is the Maxwell stress of a covariantly constant field, $G^t{}_t = G^r{}_r = -\\dfrac{1}{b^2}$ and $G^\\theta{}_\\theta = G^\\phi{}_\\phi = +\\dfrac{1}{b^2}$, which is a positive energy density $u = \\dfrac{c^4}{8\\pi G b^2}$ with a radial tension of the same size and an equal transverse pressure, the trace free stress of a radial electric field of strength $E = \\dfrac{c^2}{b\\sqrt{4\\pi G\\epsilon_0}}$.",
  "history": "In 1959 two relativists, working independently, wrote down the same unusual solution. Bruno Bertotti in Princeton and Ivor Robinson in the same year each solved the coupled Einstein-Maxwell equations for the case of a uniform electromagnetic field: a field that is the same, in a precise covariant sense, at every event [bertotti1959, robinson1959]. Most charged solutions of general relativity have a field that falls off with distance from a source; this one does not, and the price of that uniformity is paid in the geometry.¶Tullio Levi-Civita had found the same geometry in 1917, in a note to the Accademia dei Lincei on the physical reality of some of Bianchi's normal spaces, which makes it one of the earliest exact solutions of Einstein's equations; Robert Jantzen's English translation of it appeared in 2011 [levicivita1917, levicivita2011]. Bertotti, then at the Institute for Advanced Study, derived the solution by simple geometrical arguments and allowed for a cosmological constant: his manifold is the product of two surfaces of constant curvature whose type and radius depend on the cosmological constant and on the invariants of the field [bertotti1959]. He showed as well that the world lines of charged test particles in it have a simple geometrical meaning [bertotti1959].¶The resulting spacetime is conformally flat and homogeneous, and it has a clean product structure: it factorizes into an anti-de Sitter spacetime of two dimensions, carrying the time and radial directions, multiplied by a round sphere of fixed radius, written AdS$_2 \\times$ S$^2$. The electric field threads the sphere like the flux of a magnetic monopole through a closed surface, and the negative curvature of the AdS$_2$ factor exactly balances the positive curvature of the sphere, leaving a conformally flat whole with no singularity anywhere. N. Tariq and B. O. J. Tupper proved in 1974 that it is the only conformally flat solution of the source free Einstein-Maxwell equations for a field that is not null [tariq1974], and Nessim Tariq and Raymond McLenaghan gave a shorter proof in 1978 [tariq1978].¶For years the Bertotti-Robinson universe was a tidy oddity. Its importance became clear once black holes were understood: it is the limit near the horizon of an extremal Reissner-Nordström black hole, the geometry one finds deep in the throat of a charged hole pushed to the edge of having a horizon at all [reissner1916, nordstrom1918]. Gary Gibbons and Paul Townsend described the extreme Reissner-Nordström black hole in 1993, as a solution of $N = 2$ supergravity, as a soliton that runs from flat spacetime at infinity down an infinite throat to AdS$_2 \\times$ S$^2$, both of them vacua that keep all of the theory's supersymmetry [gibbons1993]. Sergio Ferrara, Renata Kallosh, and Andrew Strominger found in 1995 that at the horizon of the extremal black holes of $N = 2$ supergravity the scalar fields flow to values fixed by the charges alone, and that the geometry there is the Bertotti-Robinson universe [ferrara1995].¶James Bardeen and Gary Horowitz found the rotating counterpart in 1999: the geometry near the horizon of an extreme Kerr black hole is a nonsingular vacuum solution that shares many properties of the AdS$_2 \\times$ S$^2$ throat, among them a boundary at infinity that is timelike, and they argued that once backreaction is included AdS$_2 \\times$ S$^2$ is itself unstable [bardeen1999]. Juan Maldacena, Jeremy Michelson, and Andrew Strominger found the same year that string theory on AdS$_2 \\times$ S$^2$, unlike its analogues in higher dimensions, suppresses every excitation of finite energy, leaving configurations of zero energy that include throats branching toward the horizon and disconnected copies of the universe [maldacena1999]. Ashoke Sen related the number of microstates of an extremal black hole in 2009 to a partition function of string theory on the AdS$_2$ geometry near its horizon, which he named the quantum entropy function [sen2009]. That AdS$_2 \\times$ S$^2$ throat is now a fixture of black hole thermodynamics and of holography in low dimensions, which has kept this 1959 solution in steady use.",
